  "meta": {
    "title": "Guaymas Basin: Purple Bacteria in the Sulfide-Light Overlap",
    "date": "2026-08-01",
    "lesson": "In the Guaymas Basin hydrothermal field, hydrocarbon-rich sediments vent sulfide into a water column that still receives faint sunlight from above. Anoxygenic phototrophic sulfur bacteria exploit the narrow band where both resources overlap: too shallow and sulfide runs out, too deep and light fails. This scenario models that double dependency directly - the organism needs BOTH light and a dissolved chemical, and must hold station in the depth band where the two curves cross, exactly as a Winogradsky column stratifies into colored layers in nature.",
    "citation": "Teske et al., Guaymas Basin hydrothermal sediment microbiology, ISME J.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Guaymas Basin hydrothermal vent field, Gulf of California, ~2000m depth, purple/green sulfur bacteria at the sulfide-light overlap"
  },
